# Descent of integrality along a transcendental constant

Theorem statement: `IsIntegral.mem_span_of_adjoin_simple_constants_transcendental`
(`e0c318e5-4141-5cba-8a82-1849acf24693`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `b714685b-9ff4-5987-bf8c-c42173bc2b1e`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure, let $F$ be an intermediate field
of $\mathbb{Q} \subset \mathbb{C}$, and let $t \in K$ be transcendental over $\mathbb{C}$. Let $S
\subseteq K$ be a finite set with $t \in S$ such that every $s \in S$ is integral over the subring
of $K$ generated by the image of $F$ under the structure map $\mathbb{C} \to K$ together with $t$
(that is, over the image of $F[t]$). Let $c \in \mathbb{C}$ be transcendental over $F$, and assume
moreover that its image in $K$ is transcendental over the subfield of $K$ generated by the image of
$F$ together with $S$. Let $y \in K$ be an element which lies in the subfield of $K$ generated by
the image of the intermediate field $F(c)$ of $F \subset \mathbb{C}$ together with $S$, and which is
integral over the subring of $K$ generated by the image of $F(c)$ together with $t$. Then $y$ lies
in the $\mathbb{C}$-submodule of $K$ spanned by those $z \in K$ that simultaneously lie in the
subfield generated by the image of $F$ together with $S$ and are integral over the subring generated
by the image of $F$ together with $t$.

This is the transcendental step of a descent of the coefficient field: it removes one constant $c$,
transcendental over $F$, from the field of definition of an integral element, at the cost of passing
from a single element to a $\mathbb{C}$-linear combination. It is used, alternately with its
counterpart for algebraic constants, in the proof of
[`WLight.frickeFunction_intBaseChange`](thm.html#WLight.frickeFunction_intBaseChange).

